---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import { encounterGenerator } from '@/data/generators/encounter';
import { toolBySlug } from '@/data/tools';

const tool = toolBySlug('encounter-generator');
---

<ToolLayout tool={tool} def={encounterGenerator} guidance="Every encounter needs a reason to end.">
  <p>
    Read the situation and the objective together before anything else. Those two lines are the
    encounter. Everything under them is material to reach for when the scene needs another turn of
    the screw.
  </p>
  <h3>Hold the complication for the second beat</h3>
  <p>
    Open with the situation as written and let the players commit to a plan. The complication lands
    hardest once they have decided what they are doing, because that is the moment it costs them
    something.
  </p>
  <h3>The escalation is your timer</h3>
  <p>
    If the table stalls, or the scene has made its point and is still going, spend the escalation. It
    gives you somewhere to move without inventing anything on the spot.
  </p>
  <h3>Make the non-combat approach genuinely work</h3>
  <p>
    It is only a real choice if it succeeds. When a player finds that route, let it pay off on its
    own terms rather than routing them back into the fight.
  </p>
  <h3>Let the consequence be true</h3>
  <p>
    Walking away should mean something. Note the consequence line somewhere you will see it again,
    and let it turn up three sessions later.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>

<script>
  import { mountForge } from '@/scripts/generator-ui';
  import { encounterGenerator } from '@/data/generators/encounter';

  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-forge]');
  if (root) mountForge(root, encounterGenerator);
</script>
